// A private, per-run copy of the compiler for a harness to exec — the Bit port
// of the deleted harness `selfbin` (#1644).
//
// THE PROPERTY. A harness that runs the compiler under test must exec a copy
// that is (1) named uniquely to this run, (2) published atomically, and
// (3) proven runnable before any case depends on it. It must NEVER exec the
// build's output path directly, because that path is rewritten IN PLACE.
//
// THE FAILURE THIS REMOVES. `./make test` came back RED with every
// `[selfhost]` case failing and BOTH streams empty — a signature
// indistinguishable from "the compiler cannot build anything any more". It was
// not a compiler fault: a second build truncated and rewrote the exact file a
// live harness was exec'ing, and macOS killed the exec before `main` with
// `EXC_CRASH / SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid)`. Measured directly: exec'ing a
// binary in a loop while `cp` rewrote that same path gave 39 failures in 42
// execs (33x SIGKILL with empty output, 1x SIGBUS, 5x "cannot execute binary
// file"); the same loop against an in-place rewrite of IDENTICAL bytes gave 0
// failures in 4400 execs. Only a rewrite that truncates is fatal, which is why
// it is intermittent.
//
// WHY THIS DID NOT DIE WITH THE OLD TOOLCHAIN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THE PORT EXISTS.
// The original harness blamed its own build system: a `has_side_effects` Run step
// derives its output digest from the input hash alone, so the compiler landed at
// the same cache path every time and was rewritten in place. That reading made
// the hazard look like a property of the deleted build script. It is not. `compiler/build.bit`
// publishes a linked binary with a plain truncating `writeFile(out, ...)`
// followed by `fsChmod(out, 493)` — no temp path, no rename — so the Bit
// toolchain reproduces the hazard exactly, and it bit twice in one session:
// `_tests_/bit/stress/stress.bit`'s `privateCompiler` copied the compiler out of
// `bit-out/bin/bit` and caught a PARTIAL file mid-publish (its gate failed with
// no output at all), and `_tests_/bit/lintcmd/lintcmd.bit` exec'd that same path via
// `BIT_SELF_EXE` and died with
// "Segmentation fault: 11", empty stderr, exit 139. Both were worked around with
// build-step dependencies. Neither fixed the publish.
//
// WHAT IS CHECKABLE FROM BIT, AND WHAT IS NOT. Bit has no `stat`, no inode
// identity, no `rename` primitive and no way to observe another process opening
// a name for writing, so this file cannot watch a race. It does not need to:
// every consequence of a non-atomic publish is deterministic and is asserted
// here as a state the exec name must never be in.
//
//   * unique name          — two copies never share a path (`checkDiscipline`).
//   * atomic publish       — the exec name does not exist at all until the
//                            rename commits, and the staged file is already
//                            executable when it does (`checkAtomicity`).
//   * proven runnable      — the copy answers `--version` with exit 0 before it
//                            is returned; a torn or non-program source is
//                            REFUSED rather than handed to the corpus
//                            (`checkRefusal`).
//   * not the publish path — the copy is under /tmp and never in the directory
//                            a build writes (`checkRealCompiler`).
//   * source untouched     — publishing reads the compiler and never writes it.
//
// `checkAtomicity` also runs the contrast leg: it performs the publish
// `compiler/build.bit` performs — write the bytes to the final name, then chmod
// it — and asserts that the exec name is VISIBLE AND UNRUNNABLE in between.
// That window is not a hypothesis about that code; it is executed here.
//
// WHY /bin/sh. `osRunBounded(path, ms)` takes no argv and captures no output
// (ABI.md §19); `probe` needs both to run the compiler with `--version` and
// read its stdout back, and no Bit builtin does that. `fsRename` is a
// registered builtin now (#2712, #3266), and `fsChmod` always was, but both
// return a bare 0/-1 with no diagnostic text on failure, so `cp`, `chmod` and
// `mv` still go through the same wrapper as `probe` — exactly as
// `_tests_/bit/stress/verify.bit`'s `runScript` does — so a failed publish
// leaves stderr in a file `stepError` reads back. `exec` in a wrapper is
// load-bearing: without it the shell stays as the child and a timeout kills
// the shell rather than the process being bounded.
//
// Run it from the repository root:
//     ./bit-out/bin/bit run _tests_/bit/selfbin.bit
// `BIT_SELF_EXE` names the compiler to copy; unset, it is `<root>/bit-out/bin/bit`
// with `<root>` from `BIT_SELFBIN_ROOT` or `.`. A compiler this cannot find is a
// hard stop, never a skipped leg.
//
// WIRED INTO THE BUILD DRIVER as `test-selfbin` (`tools/build/gatestable2.bit`,
// `tools/build/defs.bit`), with the same self-hosted-install dependency every
// gate here carries.
//
// Anti-vacuity: every leg counts its assertions and `main` fails the run if
// fewer than `minChecks` of them executed, so a leg that stops checking is
// visible rather than green. The assertions were mutation-tested rather than
// trusted — five defects were injected into the routine and every one came back
// red: publishing straight to the exec name (the `build.bit` shape), a fixed
// copy name, no proof-of-life probe, a chmod moved to after the rename, and
// exec'ing the source instead of the copy. The chmod mutant SURVIVED the first
// time, which is why `checkAtomicity` builds its own 0644 fixture; see there.

// Copy attempts before giving up. A torn read of the source is a narrow race a
// re-read clears; three unrunnable copies in a row is not concurrency.
const maxAttempts: int = 3

// The name every private copy carries. Asserted, not merely used: a harness that
// exec's anything else is exec'ing something this file did not publish.
const copyPrefix: string = "/tmp/bit-selfbin-copy-"
const scratchPrefix: string = "/tmp/bit-selfbin-run-"

const defaultTimeoutS: int = 300
const clampTimeoutMs: int = 6000000

// The floor `main` enforces. 48 is what the four legs assert today; the floor
// only has to be tight enough that a whole leg going missing trips it.
const minChecks: int = 48

// 0o755. Its counterpart needs no constant: a truncating `fsOpen(path, true)`
// leaves 0o644 on its own, which is the whole point of `checkWriteThenChmod`.
const execMode: int = 493

class Ctx { scratch: string, timeoutMs: int }

class Tally { checks: int, failures: int }

class Text { ok: bool, body: string }

// One `--version` probe: whether the child exited 0, and what it printed.
class Probe { ok: bool, out: string }

// One half of a publish, and the file its shell step wrote its complaints to.
class Step { ok: bool, errPath: string }

fn say(s: string) {
  print(s)
  print("\n")
}

fn num(v: int): string {
  return "${v}"
}

fn die(s: string) {
  say("selfbin: FATAL: " + s)
  osExit(1)
}

// --- small helpers (single-file mode has no prelude and no std/*) ------------

fn indexOfAt(hay: string, needle: string, at: int): int {
  let n = len(needle)
  if (n == 0) {
    return at
  }
  let i = at
  while (i + n <= len(hay)) {
    let j = 0
    while (j < n && hay[i + j] == needle[j]) {
      j = j + 1
    }
    if (j == n) {
      return i
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}

fn startsWith(s: string, prefix: string): bool {
  return len(s) >= len(prefix) && s[:len(prefix)] == prefix
}

fn isSpace(c: u8): bool {
  return c == 32 || c == 9 || c == 13 || c == 10
}

fn trim(s: string): string {
  let a = 0
  let b = len(s)
  while (a < b && isSpace(s[a])) {
    a = a + 1
  }
  while (b > a && isSpace(s[b - 1])) {
    b = b - 1
  }
  return s[a:b]
}

// The directory part of `path`, or "." when it names no directory.
fn dirOf(path: string): string {
  let i = len(path)
  while (i > 0) {
    i = i - 1
    if (path[i] == 47) {
      return path[:i]
    }
  }
  return "."
}

// POSIX single-quoting: everything is literal inside '...', and an embedded
// quote closes, escapes and reopens.
fn shQuote(s: string): string {
  let out = "'"
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(s)) {
    if (s[i] == 39) {
      out = out + "'\\''"
    } else {
      out = out + s[i:i + 1]
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out + "'"
}

// 16 hex digits from the OS entropy pool. The clock is not enough on its own:
// two copies taken in the same nanosecond tick would collide, and uniqueness is
// half of the defence.
fn nonceHex(): string {
  let raw = cryptoRandomBytes(8)
  if (len(raw) != 8) {
    die("cryptoRandomBytes returned " + num(len(raw)) + " bytes, want 8")
  }
  let digits = "0123456789abcdef"
  let out = ""
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(raw)) {
    let b = int(raw[i])
    out = out + digits[b / 16:b / 16 + 1] + digits[b % 16:b % 16 + 1]
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

fn readText(path: string): Text {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, false)
  if (fd < 0) {
    return Text{ ok: false, body: "" }
  }
  let body = fsReadAll(fd)
  fsClose(fd)
  return Text{ ok: true, body: body }
}

// A truncating write, which is what `compiler/build.bit` publishes with. It
// leaves 0o644, so the file it produces is not executable until a later chmod.
fn writeText(path: string, body: string): bool {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, true)
  if (fd < 0) {
    return false
  }
  let n = fsWrite(fd, body)
  fsClose(fd)
  return n >= 0
}

// --- the routine under test -------------------------------------------------

// A name no other process on this box uses, drawn afresh on every attempt so a
// retry never re-uses the name that just failed.
fn uniquePath(): string {
  return copyPrefix + nonceHex()
}

fn stagingPath(path: string): string {
  return path + ".staging"
}

// Unlinks a copy — and ONLY a copy. Every caller below deletes the path
// `privateCopy` handed it, so a routine that ever returned its SOURCE instead of
// a copy would have this harness delete the compiler under test. That is not
// hypothetical: the mutation run that injected exactly that defect deleted the
// binary it was running against before the assertions could report it.
fn removeCopy(path: string) {
  if (!startsWith(path, copyPrefix)) {
    return
  }
  fsRemove(path)
}

fn runScript(cx: Ctx, path: string, body: string): int {
  if (!writeText(path, body)) {
    die("cannot write wrapper script " + path)
  }
  if (fsChmod(path, execMode) != 0) {
    die("cannot chmod wrapper script " + path)
  }
  return osRunBounded(path, cx.timeoutMs)
}

// Half one of the publish: copy to a staging name and make THAT executable. The
// exec name is untouched, so nothing can observe it in a partial state, and the
// chmod happens before the file is reachable under the name anyone execs.
//
// `cp`'s own stderr is captured rather than inherited: the refusal leg below
// expects failures, and a passing run that prints anything is a false red
// waiting to be misread — the same reason the deleted harness had a quiet voice.
fn stagePublish(cx: Ctx, src: string, path: string): Step {
  let base = cx.scratch + "/stage-" + nonceHex()
  let stage = stagingPath(path)
  let body = "#!/bin/sh\n" +
    "cp " +
    shQuote(src) +
    " " +
    shQuote(stage) +
    " 2>" +
    shQuote(base + ".err") +
    " || exit 1\n" +
    "chmod 755 " +
    shQuote(stage) +
    " 2>>" +
    shQuote(base + ".err") +
    " || exit 1\n"
  return Step{ ok: runScript(cx, base + ".sh", body) == 0, errPath: base + ".err" }
}

// Half two: rename staging onto the exec name. `mv` within /tmp is `rename(2)`,
// so a reader of `path` sees either nothing or the whole file — never a prefix.
fn commitPublish(cx: Ctx, path: string): Step {
  let base = cx.scratch + "/commit-" + nonceHex()
  let body = "#!/bin/sh\nmv " +
    shQuote(stagingPath(path)) +
    " " +
    shQuote(path) +
    " 2>" +
    shQuote(base + ".err") +
    " || exit 1\n"
  return Step{ ok: runScript(cx, base + ".sh", body) == 0, errPath: base + ".err" }
}

fn publish(cx: Ctx, src: string, path: string): Step {
  let staged = stagePublish(cx, src, path)
  if (!staged.ok) {
    return staged
  }
  return commitPublish(cx, path)
}

// What a failed publish said, for the one caller that reports rather than
// retries in silence.
fn stepError(s: Step): string {
  let t = readText(s.errPath)
  if (!t.ok || len(trim(t.body)) == 0) {
    return "(no error text)"
  }
  return trim(t.body)
}

// Proof of life, and the reason it is `--version`: it is one process start and
// one line of output, and a compiler that cannot answer it cannot build a case
// either. A torn copy fails to exec (`126`/`127` out of the wrapper), which is
// exactly what this must catch — the alternative is 93 cases blaming themselves.
fn probe(cx: Ctx, path: string): Probe {
  let base = cx.scratch + "/probe-" + nonceHex()
  let outPath = base + ".out"
  let body = "#!/bin/sh\nexec " +
    shQuote(path) +
    " --version" +
    " >" +
    shQuote(outPath) +
    " 2>" +
    shQuote(base + ".err") +
    "\n"
  let r = runScript(cx, base + ".sh", body)
  if (r == -2) {
    say("selfbin: TIMED OUT probing " + path)
  }
  if (r != 0) {
    return Probe{ ok: false, out: "" }
  }
  let t = readText(outPath)
  if (!t.ok) {
    return Probe{ ok: false, out: "" }
  }
  return Probe{ ok: true, out: t.body }
}

// Copies `src` to a path only this run names, proves the copy runs, and returns
// it — or "" when no attempt produced a runnable copy. `loud` is off for the
// refusal leg, where failure is the expected result and its diagnosis is noise.
fn privateCopy(cx: Ctx, src: string, loud: bool): string {
  let attempt = 0
  while (attempt < maxAttempts) {
    attempt = attempt + 1
    let path = uniquePath()
    let published = publish(cx, src, path)
    if (published.ok) {
      if (probe(cx, path).ok) {
        return path
      }
      if (loud) {
        say("selfbin: the copy at " + path + " is not runnable (attempt " + num(attempt) + ")")
      }
    } else if (loud) {
      say("selfbin: copying " + src + " failed (attempt " + num(attempt) + "): " + stepError(published))
    }
    removeCopy(stagingPath(path))
    removeCopy(path)
  }
  if (loud) {
    say("selfbin: no runnable copy of " + src + " in " + num(maxAttempts) + " attempts. Every case")
    say("  that depends on this compiler would have failed for that reason and not its own.")
  }
  return ""
}

// --- assertions -------------------------------------------------------------

fn check(t: Tally, cond: bool, label: string) {
  t.checks = t.checks + 1
  if (!cond) {
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
    say("FAIL: " + label)
  }
}

// Unique, whole, runnable, and leaving no staging file behind.
fn checkDiscipline(cx: Ctx, t: Tally, src: string) {
  let a = privateCopy(cx, src, true)
  let b = privateCopy(cx, src, true)
  check(t, len(a) > 0, "discipline: no private copy was produced")
  check(t, len(b) > 0, "discipline: no second private copy was produced")
  if (len(a) == 0 || len(b) == 0) {
    return
  }

  check(
    t,
    a != b,
    "discipline: two copies share the name " + a + " - one run can truncate the other's",
  )
  check(t, a != src, "discipline: the copy IS the source")
  check(t, startsWith(a, copyPrefix), "discipline: " + a + " is not under " + copyPrefix)
  check(t, startsWith(b, copyPrefix), "discipline: " + b + " is not under " + copyPrefix)
  check(t, fsExists(a), "discipline: " + a + " does not exist")
  check(t, fsExists(b), "discipline: " + b + " does not exist")
  check(t, !fsExists(stagingPath(a)), "discipline: a staging file survived at " + stagingPath(a))
  check(t, !fsExists(stagingPath(b)), "discipline: a staging file survived at " + stagingPath(b))

  let want = readText(src)
  let got = readText(a)
  check(t, want.ok && got.ok, "discipline: cannot read the source and its copy back")
  check(
    t, want.ok && got.ok && want.body == got.body,
    "discipline: the copy is not byte-identical to " + src,
  )
  check(t, probe(cx, a).ok, "discipline: the copy at " + a + " does not run")
  removeCopy(a)
  removeCopy(b)
}

// The exec name is never observable in a state that cannot be exec'd. Both
// publishes are performed for real; the assertions are about what is on disk
// between their steps.
//
// THE SOURCE HERE DELIBERATELY LACKS THE EXEC BIT, and finding that out is what
// this comment is for: `cp` copies the source's mode, so a publish that chmods
// AFTER the rename still produces an executable staged file when the source was
// already executable — the leg passed a mutant that moved the chmod, until the
// fixture stopped carrying the bit for it. 0644 is also the faithful shape:
// `compiler/build.bit`'s `writeFile` leaves exactly that, so the publisher is
// the only thing that can supply the mode.
fn checkAtomicity(cx: Ctx, t: Tally) {
  let src = cx.scratch + "/plain-source-" + nonceHex()
  check(t, writeText(src, "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n"), "atomicity: cannot write the 0644 fixture")
  check(
    t,
    !probe(cx, src).ok,
      "atomicity: the 0644 fixture is executable already, so this leg " +
      "cannot see a chmod that happens after the rename",
  )
  let p = uniquePath()
  let staged = stagePublish(cx, src, p)
  check(t, staged.ok, "atomicity: staging the copy failed: " + stepError(staged))
  check(t, fsExists(stagingPath(p)), "atomicity: staging wrote nothing")
  // The claim the whole file rests on: half a publish is invisible under the
  // name anyone execs.
  check(
    t,
    !fsExists(p),
      "atomicity: " +
      p +
      " exists before the publish committed - " +
      "a reader can catch a partial file under the exec name",
  )
  // And the exec bit is set BEFORE the name appears, not after: a chmod that
  // follows the rename leaves a window where the published name exists and
  // cannot be run.
  check(
    t,
    probe(cx, stagingPath(p)).ok,
      "atomicity: the staged file is not executable yet - " +
      "chmod must precede the rename, not follow it",
  )
  let committed = commitPublish(cx, p)
  check(t, committed.ok, "atomicity: the commit failed: " + stepError(committed))
  check(t, fsExists(p), "atomicity: the commit did not produce " + p)
  check(t, !fsExists(stagingPath(p)), "atomicity: staging survived the commit")
  check(t, probe(cx, p).ok, "atomicity: the published copy does not run")
  removeCopy(p)
  checkWriteThenChmod(cx, t, src)
  fsRemove(src)
}

// The contrast leg, and it is not hypothetical: this is exactly what
// `compiler/build.bit` does to publish a linked binary — `writeFile(out, ...)`
// then `fsChmod(out, 493)`. Between those two calls the output path exists and
// is NOT executable, so a harness that execs it in that window gets a permission
// failure, and one that COPIES it gets whatever bytes had landed so far.
fn checkWriteThenChmod(cx: Ctx, t: Tally, src: string) {
  let q = cx.scratch + "/write-then-chmod-" + nonceHex()
  let body = readText(src)
  check(t, body.ok, "write-then-chmod: cannot read " + src)
  if (!body.ok) {
    return
  }
  check(t, writeText(q, body.body), "write-then-chmod: cannot write the fixture")
  check(t, fsExists(q), "write-then-chmod: the fixture does not exist")
  check(
    t,
    !probe(cx, q).ok,
      "write-then-chmod: a truncating write left an executable file - " +
      "the 0644 window this leg demonstrates is gone, so re-check the claim about build.bit",
  )
  check(t, fsChmod(q, execMode) == 0, "write-then-chmod: chmod failed")
  check(t, probe(cx, q).ok, "write-then-chmod: the fixture does not run even after chmod")
  fsRemove(q)
}

// A source that is not a runnable program is REFUSED. Without the probe these
// copies would be handed out and every case would fail with the signature this
// file exists to prevent.
fn checkRefusal(cx: Ctx, t: Tally) {
  // A copy caught mid-publish: a real prefix of a real program. `tru` is what
  // `true` looks like truncated, and the shell exits 127 on it.
  let torn = cx.scratch + "/torn-" + nonceHex()
  check(t, writeText(torn, "#!/bin/sh\ntru"), "refusal: cannot write the torn fixture")
  check(t, fsChmod(torn, execMode) == 0, "refusal: cannot chmod the torn fixture")
  check(
    t, len(privateCopy(cx, torn, false)) == 0,
    "refusal: a torn source produced a copy - the proof-of-life probe is not running",
  )

  // Not a program at all, the `/etc/hosts` case from the deleted harness, written
  // here rather than borrowed so the leg does not depend on the host's files.
  let text = cx.scratch + "/not-a-program-" + nonceHex()
  check(
    t,
    writeText(text, "this file is not a program\n"),
    "refusal: cannot write the text fixture",
  )
  check(t, len(privateCopy(cx, text, false)) == 0, "refusal: a non-program source produced a copy")

  // A source that does not exist fails in `cp`, one step earlier, and must also
  // come back empty rather than as a path nobody can exec.
  let missing = cx.scratch + "/absent-" + nonceHex()
  check(t, !fsExists(missing), "refusal: the absent fixture exists")
  check(t, len(privateCopy(cx, missing, false)) == 0, "refusal: a missing source produced a copy")

  // The control: the same fixture, whole, IS accepted. Without this the three
  // refusals above are satisfied by a routine that refuses everything.
  let whole = cx.scratch + "/whole-" + nonceHex()
  check(t, writeText(whole, "#!/bin/sh\ntrue\nexit 0\n"), "refusal: cannot write the whole fixture")
  check(t, fsChmod(whole, execMode) == 0, "refusal: cannot chmod the whole fixture")
  let copy = privateCopy(cx, whole, true)
  check(
    t,
    len(copy) > 0,
    "refusal: a whole, runnable source was refused - the probe rejects everything",
  )
  removeCopy(copy)
  fsRemove(torn)
  fsRemove(text)
  fsRemove(whole)
}

// The same discipline against the real compiler, which is the object every other
// harness gets this wrong about. Reading `exe` is safe in a way exec'ing it is
// not: a torn read is caught by the probe, where a torn exec is a SIGKILL with
// no output.
fn checkRealCompiler(cx: Ctx, t: Tally, exe: string) {
  let before = readText(exe)
  check(t, before.ok && len(before.body) > 0, "compiler: cannot read " + exe)
  let copy = privateCopy(cx, exe, true)
  check(t, len(copy) > 0, "compiler: no runnable private copy of " + exe)
  if (len(copy) == 0) {
    return
  }

  check(t, copy != exe, "compiler: the copy IS the publish target " + exe)
  check(
    t, !startsWith(copy, dirOf(exe) + "/"),
    "compiler: the copy sits in the directory a build rewrites: " + copy,
  )
  check(t, startsWith(copy, copyPrefix), "compiler: " + copy + " is not under " + copyPrefix)

  let p = probe(cx, copy)
  check(t, p.ok, "compiler: the copy does not answer --version")
  check(
    t, indexOfAt(p.out, "bit ", 0) == 0,
    "compiler: --version printed '" + trim(p.out) + "', which is not this compiler's banner",
  )

  let after = readText(exe)
  check(
    t, after.ok && after.body == before.body,
    "compiler: publishing MODIFIED its source - a copy must never write the publish target",
  )
  let got = readText(copy)
  check(
    t, got.ok && before.ok && got.body == before.body,
    "compiler: the copy is not byte-identical to " + exe,
  )
  removeCopy(copy)
}

// --- setup ------------------------------------------------------------------

fn timeoutMs(): int {
  let raw = trim(osEnv("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S"))
  if (len(raw) == 0) {
    return defaultTimeoutS * 1000
  }
  let v = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(raw)) {
    if (raw[i] < 48 || raw[i] > 57) {
      die("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S is not a number: " + raw)
    }
    v = v * 10 + int(raw[i]) - 48
    i = i + 1
  }
  if (v == 0) {
    // docs/development.md documents 0 as "disable and block forever"; `osRunBounded` has
    // no unbounded mode and clamps to 100 minutes (ABI.md §19). Say so rather than
    // silently redefining it.
    say("selfbin: BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S=0 cannot disable the bound - using 100 minutes.")
    return clampTimeoutMs
  }
  return v * 1000
}

// The compiler to copy. `BIT_SELF_EXE` wins, exactly as `_tests_/bit/lintcmd/lintcmd.bit`
// reads it; otherwise the dev-tree artifact under `BIT_SELFBIN_ROOT` (default
// "."). Never returns "": a compiler this cannot find is a hard stop.
fn compilerUnderTest(): string {
  let explicit = trim(osEnv("BIT_SELF_EXE"))
  if (len(explicit) > 0) {
    if (!fsExists(explicit)) {
      die("BIT_SELF_EXE=" + explicit + " does not exist")
    }
    return explicit
  }
  let root = trim(osEnv("BIT_SELFBIN_ROOT"))
  if (len(root) == 0) {
    root = "."
  }
  let candidate = root + "/bit-out/bin/bit"
  if (!fsExists(candidate)) {
    die(
        "no compiler at " +
        candidate +
        " - run this from the repository root, or set " +
        "BIT_SELF_EXE / BIT_SELFBIN_ROOT",
    )
  }
  return candidate
}

fn teardown(cx: Ctx) {
  runScript(cx, cx.scratch + "/rm.sh", "#!/bin/sh\nrm -rf " + shQuote(cx.scratch) + "\n")
}

fn main() {
  let exe = compilerUnderTest()
  let scratch = scratchPrefix + nonceHex()
  if (fsMkdir(scratch) != 0) {
    die("cannot create scratch dir " + scratch)
  }
  let cx = Ctx{ scratch: scratch, timeoutMs: timeoutMs() }
  let t = Tally{ checks: 0, failures: 0 }

  // A shell script stands in for the compiler in the legs that only need "a
  // runnable file": unlike a system binary such as /bin/echo it carries no
  // platform code signature that would refuse to validate once copied out of
  // its blessed location, which is the very failure #1644 was.
  let stub = scratch + "/stub-tool-" + nonceHex()
  if (!writeText(stub, "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n")) {
    die("cannot write the stub tool " + stub)
  }
  if (fsChmod(stub, execMode) != 0) {
    die("cannot chmod the stub tool " + stub)
  }

  checkDiscipline(cx, t, stub)
  checkAtomicity(cx, t)
  checkRefusal(cx, t)
  checkRealCompiler(cx, t, exe)
  teardown(cx)

  if (t.checks < minChecks) {
    say("FAIL: only " + num(t.checks) + " checks ran, want at least " + num(minChecks))
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
  }
  if (t.failures != 0) {
    say("selfbin: FAIL - " + num(t.failures) + " of " + num(t.checks) + " checks failed")
    osExit(1)
  }
  say("selfbin: PASS - " + num(t.checks) + " checks, compiler " + exe)
}
